Maximum load power occurs when
\[
R_L=r=5.0\,\Omega
\]
The maximum power is
\[
P_{\max}=\frac{\mathcal E^2}{4r}
=\frac{(10)^2}{4(5.0)}
=5.0\,\mathrm{W}
\]

For high-efficiency delivery, the line drop is small compared with the delivery voltage, so the current is approximately
\[
I\approx\frac{P}{V}
\]
Line loss is
\[
P_{\mathrm{loss}}=I^2R_{\mathrm{line}}
\]
Substitute \(I=P/V\):
\[
P_{\mathrm{loss}}\approx\left(\frac{P}{V}\right)^2R_{\mathrm{line}}
=\frac{P^2R_{\mathrm{line}}}{V^2}
\]

Line loss scales as
\[
P_{\mathrm{loss}}\propto\frac{1}{V^2}
\]
If \(V\) is tripled,
\[
P'_{\mathrm{loss}}=\frac{1}{3^2}P_{\mathrm{loss}}
=\frac{1}{9}P_{\mathrm{loss}}
\]
The loss becomes one ninth as large.
